\section{cis window and compute cost}{

The genetic effect is split into two components (see concept doc, sec. 4):
\itemize{
\item \strong{within} (allele-specific): ALT vs REF reads inside one heterozygous
person. A read can only be assigned an allele at a variant it covers
(\code{within_reach = "read"}) or, with phasing, at a variant in the same
phase set (\code{within_reach = "phase_set"}). Its reach is therefore bounded
by read length (~tens of kb) or phase-block length, never by
\code{cis_window} alone. Cost per block ~ O(reads x variants they cover).
\item \strong{between} (dosage): needs only per-sample residual sums and genotype
dosages, i.e. O(samples x variants) per block - the same cost class as
fastQTL. This is the component that runs out to \code{cis_window}
(default 1 Mb, as in QTLtools).
}

Cost guards: variants must pass \code{min_maf} (between) or \code{min_het_carriers}
(within); at most \code{max_variants_per_block} variants closest to the block
are tested, and the number dropped is reported per block.
}

\section{Methylation calls}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"hard_fixed"} (default): a call counts only if its modkit probability
for the called state is >= \code{min_call_prob}; the SAME threshold for
every sample, chemistry and allele. Ambiguous calls are dropped and
their rate is reported per block and allele.
\item \code{"hard_modkit"}: use modkit's per-sample estimated pass threshold.
Convenient, but the threshold differs between samples/chemistries,
which biases the between component.
\item \code{"expected"}: no threshold; each call contributes its probability of
being modified (fractional k). Keeps all data, but variance is then
only approximately beta-binomial; use as sensitivity analysis.
}

\code{mod_combine} decides how 5hmC (\code{h}) is counted:
\code{"m_plus_h"} (default; equals what bisulfite arrays measure, needed for
replication against array mQTL catalogues), \code{"m_only"} (h counted as
unmodified), \code{"drop_h"} (h calls removed).
